<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koźle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cosel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koźle is a historic town on the Oder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, now part of the city of Kędzierzyn-Koźle. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of a small Silesian Piast duchy, and under Prussian rule it became a fortress, besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s allies in 1807. Its river port on the Oder later grew into one of the largest inland harbours in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kozle.jpg" alt="Koźle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LuzynaS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ko%C5%BAle,_Rynek_z_fontann%C4%85.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Koźle lies in the western part of Kędzierzyn-Koźle, where the Kłodnica flows into the Oder, about 50 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>. Its name comes from the Polish word kozioł, goat. The settlement is first mentioned in the Gesta principum Polonorum of the early 12th century, the oldest Polish chronicle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the fragmentation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Koźle was the seat of its own duchy under a local branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> from 1281 to 1355, and it received town rights in 1281. Other Piast branches ruled it until 1532, when it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>; Duke Konrad VII the White founded a monastery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Order of Friars Minor</a> there in 1431. Besieged several times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a>, it came under the Polish House of Vasa in 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breslau">Treaty of Breslau</a> in 1742, and Frederick II made it a fortress that held against Austrian sieges in 1758, 1759, 1760 and 1762. In 1807 it was besieged by the Deroy brigade of the Bavarian Army, then allied with Napoleonic France.</p><p>In the 1921 Third Silesian Uprising Polish insurgents took the part of the town east of the Oder, but it stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, the Germans ran three forced labour subcamps of Stalag VIII-B/344 there. Between August and December 1942, 39 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> trains from Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> were halted at the Cosel freight station, where about 9,600 mainly Jewish men and boys were taken off for forced labour under the Schmelt organisation; only 874 returned home. A death march from Auschwitz subcamps passed through the town in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the war Koźle passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and many of its German inhabitants were expelled. It is now a district of Kędzierzyn-Koźle, with a Roman Catholic church, a medieval chateau, the remains of the 19th-century fortress, a shipyard and an inland port.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koźle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Casimir received Bytom, an old market town in eastern Upper Silesia, together with lands around Gliwice and <b>Koźle</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bytom">Duchy of Bytom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Koźle</b> belonged to the Upper Silesian lands of the Duchy of Bytom, ruled by a branch of the Silesian Piasts.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kozle">Duchy of Koźle</a></li><li><blockquote>The Piast dukes of Oleśnica at times also held other Silesian towns, including <b>Koźle</b> and part of Bytom.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
